  "derivation": {
    "pat_vs_pad": "P pat is seṭ: patitā, patiṣyati. A pad is aniṭ: d before t gives tt in pattā, d before s gives t in patsyate. Both finite luṭ 3sg forms lack an overt personal ending but differ internally.",
    "sham": "Maitreya uses śāmyati rogaḥ, then gives śamitā. The rule that lengthens śam before śyan has no such trigger in these outer formations. The conditional reuses śamiṣy- with its own augment.",
    "nad": "The Dhātupradīpa gives both praṇadati and praṇinadati, locating the retroflex in different units across the two examples. In a two-preverb construction, the root n after ni is still dental.",
    "agreement": "P/A follows the selected agent role and actual roots, including the middle of pad. An iṭ decision belongs to the lexical/outer-affix environment, whereas retroflex ni belongs to the listed preverb/root relation."
  },
